Summary
In src/pragma.c at lines 1170 to 1175, turning off defer_foreign_keys unconditionally clears db->nDeferredImmCons and db->nDeferredCons. These two counters are what COMMIT consults to decide whether deferred foreign key violations remain. Any FK violations accumulated earlier in the transaction are erased by the pragma. A user can insert a row that breaks an FK constraint under deferred mode, toggle the pragma off, and COMMIT will succeed leaving an orphaned row in the table.

PoC
SQL script (vuln_001.sql):
CREATE TABLE parent(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY);
CREATE TABLE child(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, parent_id INTEGER REFERENCES parent(id));

INSERT INTO parent VALUES(1);

PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;

BEGIN;
PRAGMA defer_foreign_keys = ON;

-- FK violation, deferred until COMMIT
INSERT INTO child VALUES(1, 999);

-- This clears nDeferredImmCons and nDeferredCons at pragma.c:1173-1174
PRAGMA defer_foreign_keys = OFF;

-- Should fail with "FOREIGN KEY constraint failed" but succeeds
COMMIT;

SELECT 'ORPHANED_ROW_EXISTS' AS status, * FROM child WHERE parent_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM parent);
PRAGMA foreign_key_check;

Trigger output:
yuelinwang@XXF-GPU-03:/data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite$ ./sqlite3 < /data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite/audit-results/poc/src_pragma/vuln_001.sql
ORPHANED_ROW_EXISTS|1|999
child|1|parent|0

Description:
When a valid, non-null scalar subquery (SELECT DISTINCT c0 FROM t0) is used directly as a boolean condition in the WHERE clause, SQLite incorrectly evaluates the expression to false and returns an empty result set, even though the subquery returns a valid non-null value.

Version:
SQLite 3.51.2 and newer (all recent versions)

Steps to reproduce:PRAGMA cache_size = 50000;
PRAGMA temp_store=MEMORY;
PRAGMA synchronous=off;

CREATE TABLE t0 (c0 TEXT);
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS i93 ON t0(c0);
INSERT OR ABORT INTO t0 VALUES (1), (NULL), ('text');

-- The following query returns 0 rows incorrectly
SELECT * FROM t0 WHERE ((SELECT DISTINCT t0.c0 FROM t0));
Actual result:
(0 rows returned)

Expected result:
The query should return all rows in the table, because the WHERE clause contains a valid, non-null scalar subquery result, which should evaluate to TRUE.

Problem Description
When querying a cross join between a table t0 and a view v0, using a scalar subquery in the WHERE clause leads to incorrect logical evaluation:
WHERE (subquery) IS NULL → returns all rows (incorrect)
WHERE (NOT subquery) → returns all rows (incorrect)
The boolean logic is completely broken, as contradictory conditions return identical full result sets.

Reproduction SQLPRAGMA cache_size = 50000;
PRAGMA temp_store=MEMORY;
PRAGMA synchronous=off;
PRAGMA encoding = 'UTF-16';

CREATE TABLE t0 (c0 REAL , c1 BLOB , c2 BLOB );
CREATE VIEW IF NOT EXISTS v0(c0) AS
SELECT DISTINCT CAST(t0.c1 COLLATE RTRIM AS TEXT) FROM t0 LIMIT 1609474397;

CREATE INDEX i56 ON t0(
0.8860132814944123 COLLATE RTRIM ASC,
c0 ASC,
LOWER((GROUP_CONCAT(c1) IN ()))
);

