Summary
In ext/fts5/fts5_index.c, fts5PoslistFilterCallback() is invoked by fts5ChunkIterate to stream a position list across multiple FTS5 leaf pages while filtering by column. The outer scan loop guards the cursor against nChunk, but the inner varint skip does not:
do {
while( i<nChunk && pChunk[i]!=0x01 ){
while( pChunk[i] & 0x80 ) i++;      /* line 3560  no bounds check on i */
i++;
}
if( pCtx->eState ){
fts5BufferSafeAppendBlob(pCtx->pBuf, &pChunk[iStart], i-iStart);
}
...
} while( i<nChunk );

Each iteration of the inner while reads pChunk[i] and only stops when the high bit clears. On a leaf page whose position list area is entirely filled with bytes that have the high bit set (0x80 onward), the cursor walks straight past nChunk into adjacent memory until it eventually hits a byte without the continuation bit. The over-read i is then handed to fts5BufferSafeAppendBlob at line 3564, which memcpys i - iStart bytes into the output buffer that the caller allocated for only nPos + FTS5_DATA_ZERO_PADDING (about 10 bytes for a small position list). The result is an attacker-sized heap-buffer-overflow memcpy, with up to roughly one page of out-of-bounds source bytes copied into a tiny destination.

Reach requires an FTS5 column filter MATCH (MATCH 'col:term') on a .db whose %_data table has been doctored so the position list straddles a leaf boundary and the continuation page is full of 0x80 bytes. The first page advertises a small nChunk so the legitimate copy is tiny, then the iterator transitions to the continuation page where the inner loop runs away.

