Summary
In ext/misc/percentile.c, percentSort() at line 338 is the recursive median-of-three quicksort that backs the median(), percentile(), percentile_disc() and percentile_cont() aggregates:
static void percentSort(double *a, unsigned int n){       /* line 338 */
...
i = n/2;
if( a[0]>a[i] )       SWAP_DOUBLE(a[0],a[i])
else if( a[i]>a[iGt] ) SWAP_DOUBLE(a[i],a[iGt])
if( n==3 ) return;
rPivot = a[i];                                          /* pivot = median of a[0], a[n/2], a[n-1] */
...
if( iLt>=2 )      percentSort(a, iLt);                  /* line 373 */
if( n-iGt>=2 )    percentSort(a+iGt, n-iGt);            /* line 374  unbounded recursion */
}

There is no depth cap and no fallback to a non-recursive sort. The median-of-three pivot defends against the textbook worst case of an already sorted input but does not defend against an adversary who knows the algorithm and crafts an input that always produces a maximally unbalanced split.

The adversarial pattern interleaves a strictly increasing low half with a strictly decreasing high half so that at each recursion level the small end of the array contains exactly the two smallest remaining values and the rest of the array stays larger than the pivot. The pivot picked by median-of-three is always the second smallest remaining value, so the partition yields iLt=1 and iGt=2. The left sub-array has length one and stops recursing. The right sub-array has length n-2, and the same pattern reapplies. Recursion depth is therefore O(N/4) regardless of pivot selection. With N=1000000 the call chain reaches roughly 250000 frames, far beyond any sane stack.

The four aggregate functions are registered as SQLITE_INNOCUOUS, so they remain callable when TRUSTED_SCHEMA is off. Any party who can hand the victim a database whose table holds adversarial REALs, or who can issue SELECT median(x) FROM t over user-supplied numeric input, can crash the process.

