The failure address sits at the bottom of the mapped stack region and every visible frame is the same percentSort recursive call, so the crash is unambiguously the algorithm running the stack out rather than data corruption.

Fix
Add a recursion-depth parameter and switch to an iterative sort (heapsort or insertion sort on small partitions) when depth exceeds 2*log2(n). This is the standard introsort guard. A minimal change preserving the existing partition logic:
static void percentSortDepth(double *a, unsigned int n, int depth){
...
if( depth<=0 ){
percentHeapSort(a, n);     /* iterative fallback */
return;
}
...
if( iLt>=2 )      percentSortDepth(a, iLt, depth-1);
if( n-iGt>=2 )    percentSortDepth(a+iGt, n-iGt, depth-1);
}

static void percentSort(double *a, unsigned int n){
int depth = 2 * (int)log2((double)n) + 8;
percentSortDepth(a, n, depth);
}

A coarser patch is to cap recursion with if( depth>64 ) return; and accept that callers may get a partially sorted array, but the introsort variant preserves correctness for all inputs.

