In ext/misc/zipfile.c, zipfileResetCursor() walks the per-cursor pFreeEntry linked list and frees every node, but never clears the head pointer:
static void zipfileResetCursor(ZipfileCsr *pCsr){           /* line 475 */
ZipfileEntry *p;
ZipfileEntry *pNext;
pCsr->bEof = 0;
if( pCsr->pFile ){
fclose(pCsr->pFile);
pCsr->pFile = 0;
zipfileEntryFree(pCsr->pCurrent);
pCsr->pCurrent = 0;
}
for(p=pCsr->pFreeEntry; p; p=pNext){                     /* line 487 */
pNext = p->pNext;
zipfileEntryFree(p);
}
/* pCsr->pFreeEntry is NOT reset to 0 here */
}

pFreeEntry is the head of a linked list of ZipfileEntry nodes that were materialized while the vtab was scanning a particular ZIP blob. zipfileFilter() calls zipfileResetCursor() at line 1317 on every entry into the filter, then repopulates pFreeEntry at line 1334 from the new scan. zipfileClose() calls zipfileResetCursor() one more time at line 500 as the cursor is torn down.

The bug is reachable when a single cursor is reused across multiple xFilter calls with different arguments, which is exactly what the planner does for a cross-join or correlated subquery against zipfile() as a table-valued function. The sequence is:

First xFilter call receives a valid ZIP blob. zipfileLoadDirectory() walks the archive and zipfileGetEntry() mallocs a ZipfileEntry for each member. The list head is stored in pCsr->pFreeEntry.
Second xFilter call receives a non-blob argument. The early zipfileResetCursor() frees every node in the list but leaves pCsr->pFreeEntry pointing at the now-freed head. The non-blob path then takes a different branch that does not repopulate pFreeEntry.
The planner finishes the scan. zipfileClose() invokes zipfileResetCursor() a third time. The for loop at line 487 dereferences the dangling head, reading p->pNext from a freed 104-byte region, then calls zipfileEntryFree() on the same dangling pointer for a double-free.

The use-after-free read happens first because reading p->pNext precedes the call to zipfileEntryFree(p). ASAN catches the read and aborts before the double-free can land. In a non-ASAN build the double-free would corrupt the allocator's free list.

