Summary
The FTS5 snippet(table, col, before, after, ellipsis, nToken) SQL function takes the maximum-tokens-per-snippet count as its fifth argument. ext/fts5/fts5_aux.c:454 pulls it via sqlite3_value_int(apVal[4]) into a plain int nToken, and the same int value flows into two later arithmetic sites without sanitization:
/* fts5_aux.c:396 */
sqlite3_int64 iAdj = iFirst - (nToken - (iLast-iFirst)) / 2;
if( (iAdj+nToken)>nDocsize ) iAdj = nDocsize - nToken;
...
/* fts5_aux.c:547 */
ctx.iRangeStart = iBestStart;
ctx.iRangeEnd = iBestStart + nToken - 1;

When the caller passes nToken = INT_MIN, line 547 computes INT_MIN - 1 in int arithmetic and the subtraction overflows. Line 396 has a similar pattern where nToken - (iLast-iFirst) underflows. The wrapped value is then either used as a buffer offset (immediately clamped to [0, nDocsize] by the surrounding code) or fed into iRangeEnd, which only drives downstream iter.iStart < ctx.iRangeEnd comparisons rather than memory indexing. The user-visible effect is therefore a degenerate snippet (empty or odd-shaped output), not a crash or memory corruption.

This is a low-severity finding. There is no memory-safety impact under default builds, and the function still terminates normally. The reason to file it is that the C-level integer overflow is undefined behavior, it is reachable from any SQL statement, and a UBSAN-enabled deployment (which several Linux distros use for hardened SQLite builds) will abort the connection on this input.

PoC
SQL (vuln_001.sql):
CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE t USING fts5(x);
INSERT INTO t VALUES('alpha beta gamma delta');
SELECT snippet(t, 0, '<', '>', '...', -2147483648) FROM t WHERE t MATCH 'beta';

Run:
./sqlite3 < vuln_001.sql

UBSAN output:
sqlite3.c:244090:43: runtime error: signed integer overflow: -2147483648 - 1 cannot be represented in type 'int'

The file/line in the amalgamation corresponds to ext/fts5/fts5_aux.c:547, which is the iBestStart + nToken - 1 expression. A second UBSAN report fires on line 396 if the same query exercises the multi-phrase-match branch. The trigger requires only the default FTS5 tokenizer and a single row.

Fix
Either reject pathological nToken values at the entry point or widen the arithmetic to 64-bit so the subtraction cannot overflow. The narrow fix at fts5_aux.c:454:
nToken = sqlite3_value_int(apVal[4]);
if( nToken<1 || nToken>1000 ) nToken = 15;          /* same clamp the default uses elsewhere */

A broader fix is to declare nToken as sqlite3_int64 throughout the function and cast back to int only at the final result emission, which removes both the line 396 and line 547 overflow sites without changing the documented snippet behavior on legitimate inputs.

Summary
sqlite3Fts5HashWrite() at ext/fts5/fts5_hash.c:245 buffers a per-term doclist in an Fts5HashEntry whose nAlloc field is declared as int at fts5_hash.c:68. When the entry needs to grow, line 326 doubles the size in int arithmetic before widening:
if( (p->nAlloc - p->nData) < (9 + 4 + 1 + 3 + 5) ){
sqlite3_int64 nNew = p->nAlloc * 2;            /* line 326, int*int then widen */
Fts5HashEntry *pNew;
...
pNew = (Fts5HashEntry*)sqlite3_realloc64(p, nNew);
...
pNew->nAlloc = (int)nNew;
}

When nAlloc reaches 0x40000000 (2^30), 1073741824 * 2 overflows the signed int and the doubling result is undefined before it is assigned to nNew. Reachability depends on push the hash entry past 2^30 bytes inside a single document. Ordinary tokenizers spend at least 2 input bytes per token (token + separator), so under the default SQLITE_MAX_LENGTH = 1000000000 the doclist stays below the threshold. The trigram tokenizer emits roughly one token per input byte for a run of identical characters, and the project build raises the limit (-DSQLITE_MAX_LENGTH=2100000000 per targets/readme.md), so a single ~1.08e9-byte run of 'a' accumulates a >2^30 byte doclist in the hash entry and drives the doubling into the overflow. The hardened build also enables -fsanitize=signed-integer-overflow -fno-sanitize-recover=all, so the UB aborts the process under UBSAN. Under a plain release the wrapped negative result is rejected by sqlite3_realloc64, the INSERT fails with SQLITE_NOMEM, and no memory corruption is observed.

PoC
SQL (vuln_001.sql):
CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE t USING fts5(x, tokenize=trigram);
INSERT INTO t VALUES(printf('%.*c', 1080000000, 'a'));

printf('%.*c', 1080000000, 'a') constructs a 1.08e9-byte string of 'a' directly inside sqlite3_str_appendf, which sizes the buffer with i64 arithmetic and is safe. The trigram tokenizer then emits roughly one aaa position per input byte, accumulating a >2^30-byte doclist in the Fts5HashEntry, and the next sqlite3Fts5HashWrite() call crosses the doubling threshold. The more direct-looking replace(hex(zeroblob(540000000)),'0','a') formulation does NOT reach this path because it trips an earlier int overflow in replaceFunc() (src/func.c) at the realloc step (int)nOut + (nOut - nStr - 1), where both operands are also near 1e9.

Run on a build with -DSQLITE_MAX_LENGTH=2100000000 -fsanitize=signed-integer-overflow -fno-sanitize-recover=all:
./sqlite3 < vuln_001.sql

UBSAN output:
sqlite3.c:249667:38: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 1073741824 * 2 cannot be represented in type 'int'

sqlite3.c:249667 maps to ext/fts5/fts5_hash.c:326 in the amalgamation. UBSAN aborts the connection because of -fno-sanitize-recover=all.

Fix
Widen the operand before multiplying so the product is computed in 64-bit:
sqlite3_int64 nNew = (sqlite3_int64)p->nAlloc * 2;

The same int * 2 doubling pattern on attacker-influenced poslist sizes lives at ext/fts5/fts5_index.c:4875, 5733, and 5984 (two nPos*2 and one nData*2). Apply the same widening cast to each. None of these sites are reachable under the default SQLITE_MAX_LENGTH, but they all become reachable once the limit is raised past ~2^30.

Summary
fts5IndexTombstoneRebuild() at ext/fts5/fts5_index.c:8018-8025 takes the tombstone hash element count straight from the on-page bytes and multiplies by 4 in int arithmetic to size the new slot array:
}else if( pSeg->nPgTombstone==1 ){
/* Case 2. */
int nElem = (int)fts5GetU32(&pData1->p[4]);   /* line 8020  attacker-controlled */
assert( pData1 && iPg1==0 );
nOut = 1;
nSlot = MAX(nElem*4, MINSLOT);                /* line 8023  signed-int overflow */
if( nSlot>nSlotPerPage ) nOut = 0;
}

A tombstone page whose bytes 4-7 are >= 0x20000000 (536870912) makes nElem*4 exceed INT_MAX and the multiplication overflows. Reachable from a single DELETE against any contentless_delete=1 FTS5 table whose tombstone page has been doctored, either by a malicious .db file or by a shadow-table write under non-DEFENSIVE mode. On the project build (--enable-debug -fsanitize=signed-integer-overflow -fno-sanitize-recover=all) UBSAN aborts the process. On a release build the wrapped value is rescued by the MINSLOT=32 floor and the nSlot>nSlotPerPage guard, so release is undefined-but-benign.

PoC
vuln_001_gen.py builds a small contentless_delete FTS5 table with one tombstone page and then rewrites bytes 4-7 of that page to 0x20000000:
