Summary
delta_create() in ext/misc/fossildelta.c:372-376 allocates the collide hash table and then immediately writes to it without checking the return value:
nHash = lenSrc/NHASH;                                          /* NHASH=16 */
collide = sqlite3_malloc64( (sqlite3_int64)nHash*2*sizeof(int) );
memset(collide, -1, nHash*2*sizeof(int));                      /* line 373, NULL deref on OOM */
landmark = &collide[nHash];
for(i=0; i<lenSrc-NHASH; i+=NHASH){
...
collide[i/NHASH] = landmark[hv];                             /* further deref */
}

The allocation size is nHash * 2 * sizeof(int) = lenSrc/2, which scales linearly with the attacker-controlled source length. When sqlite3_malloc64() returns NULL (OOM, or hit SQLITE_LIMIT_LENGTH), the subsequent memset(NULL, -1, ...) writes to address 0 and the process crashes. SQLite's documented contract is that an OOM at any allocation site must surface as SQLITE_NOMEM to the caller and never crash, so this is an OOM-robustness defect.

Reachable from a single SELECT delta_create(src, target) against the fossildelta extension (ext/misc/fossildelta.c:662 deltaCreateFunc), once the extension is loaded with .load or compiled in. The realistic real-world failure path is a lenSrc close to SQLITE_LIMIT_LENGTH on a memory-constrained host where the matching lenSrc/2 allocation fails. For deterministic reproduction we install a public SQLITE_CONFIG_MALLOC wrapper that fails allocations in a specific size band, which is the same mechanism the SQLite test suite uses for OOM-injection coverage.

PoC
vuln_001_mock_oom.c installs an sqlite3_mem_methods wrapper via SQLITE_EXTRA_INIT that delegates everything to the system allocator except allocations whose size is between OOM_FAIL_MIN and OOM_FAIL_MAX (env vars), for which it returns NULL. Then the standard sqlite3 shell drives the trigger SQL.

Build (shell + amalgamation + fossildelta + the mock allocator, all in one binary):
