Summary
realpathFunc() at ext/misc/fileio.c:1186-1190 is the SQL backend of the realpath(X) scalar function. It copies the input path with sqlite3_mprintf and then immediately calls strlen and indexes the buffer without checking for OOM:
zPath = (const char*)sqlite3_value_text(argv[0]);
if( zPath==0 ) return;
if( zPath[0]==0 ) zPath = ".";
zCopy = sqlite3_mprintf("%s", zPath);            /* line 1189, may be NULL */
len = strlen(zCopy);                              /* line 1190, strlen(NULL) -> SEGV */
while( len>1 && (zCopy[len-1]=='/' || ...) ){     /* zCopy[len-1] further deref */
len--;
}
zCopy[len] = 0;

sqlite3_mprintf returns NULL on allocation failure. Unlike the expert.c:56 fread(NULL, ...) case where the kernel returns EFAULT for large reads, strlen(NULL) dereferences address 0 immediately for any input size. The allocation size scales with the attacker-controlled path length, so under genuine memory pressure with a long path argument the mprintf is realistic to fail. SQLite's documented contract is that OOM must surface as SQLITE_NOMEM rather than a crash, so this is an OOM-robustness defect.

Reachable from a single SELECT realpath(?) against the fileio extension (ext/misc/fileio.c, the common readfile/writefile/fsdir/realpath bundle) once it is loaded with .load or compiled in.

PoC
Triggered through the official sqlite3 shell. realpath() is built into shell.c (registered by the embedded sqlite3_fileio_init), so no .load is needed. The only auxiliary code is a small mock allocator that hooks the public SQLITE_CONFIG_MALLOC API and exposes an oom_arm(lo, hi, skip) SQL function so the failure window can be opened from inside the same shell session.

