Summary
I found a heap-buffer-overflow in the FTS3/FTS4 NEAR query path. The crash is reachable from the stock sqlite3 shell when a database contains a crafted FTS shadow-table row.

The relevant code is in ext/fts3/fts3.c. fts3EvalNearTrim() trims one phrase's position list by writing the result of fts3PoslistNearMerge() back into pPhrase->doclist.pList:
/* ext/fts3/fts3.c */
p2 = pOut = pPhrase->doclist.pList;
res = fts3PoslistNearMerge(
&pOut, aTmp, nParam1, nParam2, paPoslist, &p2
);
if( res ){
nNew = (int)(pOut - pPhrase->doclist.pList) - 1;
assert_fts3_nc( nNew<=pPhrase->doclist.nList && nNew>0 );
if( nNew>=0 && nNew<=pPhrase->doclist.nList ){
memset(&pPhrase->doclist.pList[nNew], 0, pPhrase->doclist.nList - nNew);
pPhrase->doclist.nList = nNew;
}
}

The size check is performed after the merge has already written into the original doclist.pList buffer. fts3PoslistMerge() also documents that its caller must ensure the output buffer is large enough:
/* ext/fts3/fts3.c */
** The caller is responsible for insuring
** that there is enough space in *pp to hold the complete output.

With a malformed FTS4 t_segdir.root value, the NEAR merge can copy more bytes than fit in the right-hand position-list buffer. In my test case, the overflow happens in fts3ColumnlistCopy() while it is called from fts3PoslistMerge() / fts3PoslistNearMerge() / fts3EvalNearTrim().

This requires corrupt or malicious FTS shadow-table state. The SQL below uses .dbconfig defensive off only so the shell can insert that shadow-table row directly; an equivalent malicious database file could contain the same row. I have demonstrated a heap overwrite and crash under ASan, not a control-flow exploit.

