Summary
I found a signed integer overflow in the FTS3 segment promotion path. The crash is reachable from the stock sqlite3 shell when a database contains a crafted %_segdir.end_block text value.

The relevant parser is fts3ReadEndBlockField() in ext/fts3/fts3_write.c. The comment says an end_block text field may contain two non-negative integers, but the implementation accepts a minus sign for the second field and then multiplies the parsed value by -1:
/* ext/fts3/fts3_write.c */
u64 iVal = 0;
for(i=0; zText[i]>='0' && zText[i]<='9'; i++){
iVal = iVal*10 + (zText[i] - '0');
}
*piEndBlock = (i64)iVal;
while( zText[i]==' ' ) i++;
iVal = 0;
if( zText[i]=='-' ){
i++;
iMul = -1;
}
for(/* no-op */; zText[i]>='0' && zText[i]<='9'; i++){
iVal = iVal*10 + (zText[i] - '0');
}
*pnByte = ((i64)iVal * (i64)iMul);

For the text value 0 -9223372036854775808, the second parsed iVal becomes 9223372036854775808. Casting that u64 to i64 produces INT64_MIN on the tested platform, and multiplying it by -1 triggers signed integer overflow.

The PoC below places that text in t_segdir.end_block at level 1, then performs a normal FTS3 insert. During transaction sync, sqlite3Fts3PendingTermsFlush() calls fts3SegmentMerge(), then fts3PromoteSegments(), and promotion scans the crafted row with fts3ReadEndBlockField().

This requires corrupt or malicious FTS shadow-table state. The SQL uses .dbconfig defensive off only so the shell can insert that shadow-table row directly; an equivalent malicious database file could contain the same value. I am reporting this as undefined behavior in a reachable FTS3 maintenance path, not as a demonstrated memory corruption primitive.

