Summary
In ext/fts5/fts5_expr.c at fts5ParseTokenize() lines 1758 to 1764, the synonym branch reads from pSyn->pTerm before the source bytes are copied into it. The nQueryTerm field controls how many leading bytes of the term feed prefix matching, so the post-bug value of zero turns any prefix query against a synonymized stem into a match-everything scan.
memset(pSyn, 0, (size_t)nByte);                   /* 1758  buffer zeroed     */
pSyn->pTerm = ((char*)pSyn) + sizeof(Fts5ExprTerm) /* 1759  points into zeros */
+ sizeof(Fts5Buffer);
pSyn->nFullTerm = pSyn->nQueryTerm = nToken;       /* 1760  correct value     */
if( pCtx->pConfig->bTokendata ){
pSyn->nQueryTerm = (int)strlen(pSyn->pTerm);   /* 1762  BUG strlen == 0   */
}
memcpy(pSyn->pTerm, pToken, nToken);               /* 1764  too late          */

The strlen at line 1762 walks the zero-filled region left over from line 1758 and returns 0, overwriting the correct nToken written at line 1760. The actual token bytes are copied in only one line later at 1764. The non-synonym sibling at lines 1789 and 1792 escapes the bug because there pTerm->pTerm is initialised by sqlite3Fts5Strndup(), which allocates and populates the buffer before any strlen runs.

The bug triggers when (1) the FTS5 table was created with tokendata=1, (2) a custom tokenizer registered through fts5_api.xCreateTokenizer() emits FTS5_TOKEN_COLOCATED during query tokenization, and (3) the query is a prefix MATCH such as word* whose stem is a synonymized term. None of the built-in tokenizers (unicode61, porter, trigram, ascii) emit colocated tokens, so the bug is not reachable from the stock sqlite3 CLI. It affects any embedder that links FTS5 and registers a synonym-capable tokenizer through the C API on tables created with tokendata=1.

With nQueryTerm=0 the synonym leg of the OR-merged iterator walks every term in the FTS5 index. OR'd against the primary term, the result set absorbs every document regardless of content, so the MATCH predicate is effectively bypassed.

