Summary
In src/alter.c at dropConstraintFunc() lines 2586 to 2601, the tokenizer walk that locates the byte range to splice out of the stored schema text fails to advance iOff past the constraint value when the constraint keyword is DEFAULT, COLLATE or GENERATED. The function reports success, but the splice removes only the CONSTRAINT <name> label and leaves the value intact.
nTok = getConstraintToken(&zSql[iOff], &t);                  /* 2586  peek next */
if( t==TK_CONSTRAINT || t==TK_DEFAULT || t==TK_COLLATE
|| t==TK_COMMA || t==TK_RP || t==TK_GENERATED || t==TK_AS
){
t = TK_CHECK;                                              /* 2590  retype  */
/* BUG  iOff NOT advanced past the constraint value */
}else{
iOff += nTok;
iOff += getConstraint(&zSql[iOff]);                        /* 2593  normal  */
}

if( cmp==0 || (iNotNull>=0 && t==TK_NOT) ){
if( t!=TK_NOT && t!=TK_CHECK ){
errorMPrintf(ctx, "constraint may not be dropped: %s", zCons);
return;
}
iEnd = iOff;                                               /* 2601  splice end */
break;
}

The non-CHECK branch on the else side at line 2592 advances iOff by both the keyword length and the parsed constraint body, so iEnd = iOff correctly bounds the value. The CHECK-retype branch at line 2590 only relabels the token and breaks out of the loop without advancing, so iEnd is left pointing at the byte just before the constraint value rather than just after it. The resulting splice removes CONSTRAINT <name> but preserves DEFAULT 5, COLLATE NOCASE, or GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) VIRTUAL verbatim.

Three named-constraint forms are affected: DEFAULT, COLLATE, and GENERATED. All other forms (PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, CHECK, NOT NULL, REFERENCES) take the else branch at line 2591 and remove the constraint cleanly.

DROP CONSTRAINT has been a stable feature since SQLite 3.35 (2021). The operation reports 0 rows affected and no error, so a caller has no signal that the schema was not modified as requested. The schema can only be returned to the intended state through a CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT rebuild or by editing sqlite_master via PRAGMA writable_schema=ON.

PoC
Run the three test cases through the stock sqlite3 binary. No ASAN, no extension, no special flags. The bug is visible in the post-DROP sqlite_master.sql text.
/data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite/install/bin/sqlite3 <<'SQL'
CREATE TABLE t1(a TEXT CONSTRAINT mydefault DEFAULT 5);
ALTER TABLE t1 DROP CONSTRAINT mydefault;
SELECT sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE name='t1';

CREATE TABLE t2(a TEXT CONSTRAINT mycollate COLLATE NOCASE);
ALTER TABLE t2 DROP CONSTRAINT mycollate;
SELECT sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE name='t2';

CREATE TABLE t3(a INT, b INT CONSTRAINT mygen GENERATED ALWAYS AS (a*2) VIRTUAL);
ALTER TABLE t3 DROP CONSTRAINT mygen;
SELECT sql FROM sqlite_master WHERE name='t3';
SQL

Output on SQLite 3.54.0:
CREATE TABLE t1(a TEXT DEFAULT 5)
CREATE TABLE t2(a TEXT COLLATE NOCASE)
CREATE TABLE t3(a INT, b INT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (a*2) VIRTUAL)

Expected output is CREATE TABLE t1(a TEXT), CREATE TABLE t2(a TEXT), CREATE TABLE t3(a INT, b INT). The CONSTRAINT mydefault / CONSTRAINT mycollate / CONSTRAINT mygen labels are correctly removed, but the value that the label introduced stays in place.

Follow-up INSERT confirms the retained DEFAULT is still active at write time, not just present in the schema string:
/data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite/install/bin/sqlite3 <<'SQL'
CREATE TABLE t1(a TEXT CONSTRAINT mydefault DEFAULT 5);
ALTER TABLE t1 DROP CONSTRAINT mydefault;
INSERT INTO t1 DEFAULT VALUES;
SELECT a FROM t1;
SQL

Output is 5, demonstrating that the DEFAULT clause was not removed in semantic effect either, only in the CONSTRAINT label.

Fix
Advance iOff past the constraint value in the CHECK-retype branch so the splice at line 2601 covers the value. The advance mirrors the else branch at lines 2592 to 2593.
nTok = getConstraintToken(&zSql[iOff], &t);
if( t==TK_CONSTRAINT || t==TK_DEFAULT || t==TK_COLLATE
|| t==TK_COMMA || t==TK_RP || t==TK_GENERATED || t==TK_AS
){
t = TK_CHECK;
iOff += nTok;
iOff += getConstraint(&zSql[iOff]);
}else{
iOff += nTok;
iOff += getConstraint(&zSql[iOff]);
}

The two branches now both advance, so the conditional could be simplified to compute iOff unconditionally and only the t reclassification stays inside the if. A reviewer should also confirm that getConstraint() returns the right span for the GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) [VIRTUAL|STORED] form, which is the longest of the three affected values.

Summary
In ext/misc/series.c at seriesFilter() lines 533 to 542, the float branch for value >= X / value > X lacks the upper-bound clamp that the symmetric <= X / < X branch at line 557 has. With r > LARGEST_INT64, the (sqlite3_int64)r cast is UB (C 6.3.1.4) and yields INT64_MIN on x86-64. iMin becomes INT64_MIN, which never raises iBase in the merge at line 584. xBestIndex sets omit=1 on the constraint, so SQLite does not re-filter, and every row of the series is returned despite the WHERE.
/* GE/GT, BUG: no r > LARGEST_INT64 clamp */
if( r<(double)SMALLEST_INT64 ){ iMin = SMALLEST_INT64; }
else if( ... )                 { iMin = (sqlite3_int64)seriesCeil(r)+1; }
else                           { iMin = (sqlite3_int64)seriesCeil(r);   }

/* LE/LT, correct */
if( r>(double)LARGEST_INT64 )  { iMax = LARGEST_INT64; }

generate_series() is built into the CLI shell and into the amalgamation under SQLITE_ENABLE_GENERATE_SERIES (default since 3.43), so the vtab is reachable from any SQL session with no load and no flags.

PoCecho "SELECT value FROM generate_series(1) WHERE value >= 1e19 LIMIT 3;" \
