Root Cause Analysis
When the parser encounters +column_name, it creates a TK_UPLUS expression node wrapping the column reference. The sqlite3ExprAffinity() function is called by the query planner and code generator to determine type affinity for comparisons and type coercion. Because it doesn't traverse through TK_UPLUS, it returns SQLITE_AFF_BLOB (no affinity) instead of the column's actual affinity (e.g., SQLITE_AFF_TEXT).

This affects binaryCompareP5() (expr.c:404) and comparisonAffinity() (expr.c:370), which determine the P5 flags for VDBE comparison operations. With wrong P5 flags, the VDBE performs comparisons with incorrect type coercion:

TEXT affinity (correct): Converts numeric values to text for string comparison
BLOB affinity (buggy): No conversion; uses SQLite's type ordering where INTEGER < TEXT

The structural pattern matches the DuckDB PEG parser bug class: an expression property function fails to "look through" a wrapper operator (unary +), causing the property to be incorrectly computed. In DuckDB it was AST structure; in SQLite it is type affinity.

PoC-- sqlite3ExprAffinity() UPLUS Bug: Wrong Query Results
-- The unary plus (+) operator is documented as a no-op in SQLite:
--   "It can be applied to strings, numbers, blobs or NULL and it
--    always returns a result with the same value as the operand."
-- But due to the bug, +column changes comparison semantics by
-- stripping the column's affinity.

CREATE TABLE t(a TEXT);
INSERT INTO t VALUES('0123');

-- CORRECT: a > 123 uses TEXT affinity
-- Integer 123 is converted to text '123', then string comparison:
-- '0123' vs '123' → '0' < '1' → '0123' < '123' → FALSE (0)
SELECT a > 123 AS correct FROM t;
-- Expected: 0    Actual: 0 ✓

-- BUGGY: +a > 123 uses BLOB affinity (no conversion)
-- Type ordering: INTEGER(123) < TEXT('0123') → TRUE (1)
SELECT +a > 123 AS buggy FROM t;
-- Expected: 0    Actual: 1 ✗  WRONG RESULT!

-- Both should return the same result since + is documented as a no-op.
-- Proof that UPLUS preserves the stored value and type:
SELECT typeof(a), typeof(+a) FROM t;
-- Both return 'text'

-- Additional wrong results:
SELECT a < 123 AS correct FROM t;     -- 1 (TRUE): '0123' < '123' as strings ✓
SELECT +a < 123 AS buggy FROM t;      -- 0 (FALSE): wrong! TEXT > INTEGER ✗

SELECT a BETWEEN 0 AND 200 AS correct FROM t;   -- 1: TEXT compare, '0123' >= '0' AND '0123' <= '200' ✓
SELECT +a BETWEEN 0 AND 200 AS buggy FROM t;     -- 0: WRONG! BLOB: TEXT <= INT is FALSE ✗

SELECT CASE WHEN a > 100 THEN 'yes' ELSE 'no' END FROM t;   -- 'no' ✓
SELECT CASE WHEN +a > 100 THEN 'yes' ELSE 'no' END FROM t;  -- 'yes' ✗

-- CREATE TABLE AS gets wrong column affinity:
CREATE TABLE t_ok AS SELECT a FROM t;
CREATE TABLE t_bug AS SELECT +a AS a FROM t;
INSERT INTO t_ok VALUES(456);
INSERT INTO t_bug VALUES(456);
SELECT typeof(a), a FROM t_ok;   -- text|456  (correctly coerced to text)
SELECT typeof(a), a FROM t_bug;  -- integer|456  (BUG: stored as integer, not coerced)

-- COUNT(*) exacerbates the impact:
CREATE TABLE products(price TEXT);
INSERT INTO products VALUES('9.99'), ('19.99'), ('29.99');
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE price > 10;    -- 3 (TEXT compare: all > '10')
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE +price > 10;   -- 3 (BLOB: TEXT > INT, all true)
-- These happen to give the same result, but for DIFFERENT reasons:
-- TEXT: '9.99' > '10' is TRUE (string: '9' > '1'), '19.99' > '10' is TRUE, etc.
-- BLOB: all TEXT values > any INTEGER (type ordering)

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE price < 15;    -- 1 (only '9.99' < '15' as strings... wait)
-- Actually with TEXT affinity: '9.99' < '15' → FALSE ('9' > '1'), '19.99' < '15' → FALSE
-- So 0 rows match
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE +price < 15;   -- 0 (BLOB: no TEXT < INTEGER)

Fix
Add TK_UPLUS traversal to sqlite3ExprAffinity(), consistent with sqlite3ExprCollSeq() and sqlite3ExprDataType():
// In src/expr.c, function sqlite3ExprAffinity(), add after TK_CAST handling:
if( op==TK_CAST || op==TK_UPLUS ){
pExpr = pExpr->pLeft;
op = pExpr->op;
continue;
}

For TK_UMINUS, which always produces a numeric result, the correct handling would be:
if( op==TK_UMINUS ){
return SQLITE_AFF_NUMERIC;
}

Summary
In src/whereexpr.c at exprAnalyzeOrTerm() lines 893-906, the guard loop that decides whether to rewrite col=a OR col=b into col IN (a,b) checks affinity compatibility (lines 899-902) but performs no collation check. When the common column is on the indexed table side (e.g. t2.val with BINARY collation), the optimizer commutes each OR branch so t2.val becomes the left operand, then synthesizes t2.val IN (t1.a, t1.b) at lines 926-932. The resulting IN expression inherits t2.val's BINARY collation, discarding the NOCASE collation from t1.a/t1.b that governed the original individual comparisons. This causes the query to return fewer rows (or zero rows) compared to the un-optimized path.

The EP_Collate guard at line 1319 only fires when an explicit COLLATE operator appears in the TK_OR expression itself. It does not protect against schema-declared column collations (CREATE TABLE ... COLLATE NOCASE), which live on TK_COLUMN nodes without the EP_Collate flag.

PoC
SQL script (vuln_068.sql):
-- Setup
CREATE TABLE t1(a TEXT COLLATE NOCASE, b TEXT COLLATE NOCASE);
CREATE TABLE t2(val TEXT);
CREATE INDEX t2_idx ON t2(val);
INSERT INTO t1 VALUES('Hello', 'World');
INSERT INTO t2 VALUES('hello');
INSERT INTO t2 VALUES('world');

-- Test 1: individual conditions work correctly (NOCASE match)
SELECT 'TEST1_single_a' AS test, t2.val FROM t1, t2 WHERE t1.a = t2.val;
-- Expected: hello
SELECT 'TEST1_single_b' AS test, t2.val FROM t1, t2 WHERE t1.b = t2.val;
-- Expected: world

-- Test 2: OR triggers OR-to-IN, loses NOCASE -> 0 rows (BUG)
SELECT 'TEST2_or_with_index' AS test, t2.val FROM t1, t2
WHERE t1.a = t2.val OR t1.b = t2.val;
-- Expected: hello, world (2 rows)
-- Actual:   (0 rows)

-- Test 3: same query without index -> no OR-to-IN -> 2 rows (correct)
DROP INDEX t2_idx;
SELECT 'TEST3_or_no_index' AS test, t2.val FROM t1, t2
WHERE t1.a = t2.val OR t1.b = t2.val;
-- Expected: hello, world (2 rows)
-- Actual:   hello, world (2 rows, correct)

Trigger output:
TEST1_single_a|hello
TEST1_single_b|world
TEST3_or_no_index|hello
TEST3_or_no_index|world

Note: TEST2 produces zero output rows. TEST1 and TEST3 return correct results. The query plan for TEST2 shows SEARCH t2 USING COVERING INDEX t2_idx (val=?), confirming the OR-to-IN rewrite.

Root Cause
In exprAnalyzeOrTerm(), the validation loop at lines 883-907 checks whether all OR branches share a common left column and compatible affinities:
// src/whereexpr.c:893-906
}else{
int affLeft, affRight;
/* If the right-hand side is also a column, then the affinities
** of both right and left sides must be such that no type
** conversions are required on the right.  (Ticket #2249)
*/
affRight = sqlite3ExprAffinity(pOrTerm->pExpr->pRight);
affLeft = sqlite3ExprAffinity(pOrTerm->pExpr->pLeft);
if( affRight!=0 && affRight!=affLeft ){
okToChngToIN = 0;
}else{
pOrTerm->wtFlags |= TERM_OK;
}
}

This checks affinity but not collation. When the check passes, the IN expression is synthesized at lines 926-932:
// src/whereexpr.c:926-932
pDup = sqlite3ExprDup(db, pOrTerm->pExpr->pRight, 0);  // RHS values
pList = sqlite3ExprListAppend(pWInfo->pParse, pList, pDup);
pLeft = pOrTerm->pExpr->pLeft;                          // LHS = t2.val (commuted)
...
pDup = sqlite3ExprDup(db, pLeft, 0);    // LHS of IN = t2.val
pNew = sqlite3PExpr(pParse, TK_IN, pDup, 0);

The synthesized t2.val IN (t1.a, t1.b) naturally uses t2.val's BINARY collation because pLeft is the post-commute LHS. The original NOCASE collation from t1.a/t1.b (which determined the comparison outcome before commutation) is simply discarded.

Impact
Wrong query results. Queries using OR across columns with different collations silently return fewer rows when an index enables the OR-to-IN optimization. The same query returns correct results without the index. This is a correctness bug that can cause data loss in application logic relying on case-insensitive matching.

Fix
Add a collation check in the validation loop at line 893, alongside the existing affinity check. If the effective collation for the OR term's comparison differs from the first term's collation, set okToChngToIN = 0. Alternatively, wrap the synthesized IN's LHS in a TK_COLLATE node that preserves the correct collation from the original terms. Incomplete fix about https://sqlite.org/src/info/622882529558b477.

Summary
ext/fts3/fts3_write.c:4362, (int) cast applied to an i64 block range before dividing.
pWriter->nLeafEst = (int)((iEnd - iStart) + 1)/FTS_MAX_APPENDABLE_HEIGHT;

iEnd and iStart come from %_segdir.end_block / start_block (both i64). When iEnd-iStart+1 >= 2^31 the cast wraps negative, division by 16 leaves nLeafEst negative, and the gate at line 4073 pLeaf->iBlock < (pWriter->iStart + pWriter->nLeafEst) is permanently false. No leaf flush fires during the incremental merge, so merged data lands on iStart + i*nLeafEst (line 4369) which steps backward from iStart and may overwrite existing segment blocks. With end_block = start_block + 2^31 - 1, nLeafEst = -134217728 (correct +134217728).

