Description
The ordering terms ORDER BY x, x are equivalent to ORDER BY x. SQLite does not remove the duplicate term when planning the query.

Expected behavior
Duplicate ordering expressions should be canonicalized before access-path selection. An index ordered by x should satisfy both forms without a temporary sort.

Actual behavior
ORDER BY x uses an index scan directly. ORDER BY x, x adds:
USE TEMP B-TREE FOR LAST TERM OF ORDER BY

On 400,000 rows, the duplicate-term query was approximately 1.45× slower (181.222 ms versus 261.919 ms).

How to repeatSELECT sqlite_version();

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t;
CREATE TABLE t(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, x INTEGER, y INTEGER);
CREATE INDEX idx_t_x ON t(x);

WITH RECURSIVE seq(i) AS (
SELECT 0 UNION ALL SELECT i + 1 FROM seq WHERE i < 399999
)
INSERT INTO t SELECT i, i % 200000, i % 10 FROM seq;

EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN
SELECT x, y FROM t ORDER BY x;

EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN
SELECT x, y FROM t ORDER BY x, x;

Both queries return the same rows in the same order. The second plan should report a temporary B-tree for the last ordering term.

VersionSQLite 3.50.4

Description
EXISTS only tests whether at least one row exists; ordering rows inside the subquery cannot affect its boolean result. SQLite nevertheless retains the inner ORDER BY when selecting the access path.

Expected behavior
SQLite should remove ORDER BY from an EXISTS subquery without LIMIT or window semantics and keep the covering-index lookup.

Actual behavior
Without the inner order, SQLite uses:
SEARCH b USING COVERING INDEX idx_t_x (x=?)

With the redundant order, it uses:
SEARCH b USING INDEX idx_t_x (x=?)

The table is no longer covered, causing row lookups. In a 400,000-row test, the ordered form was 1.18× slower (61.364 ms versus 72.425 ms).

