Optimization: Specialized sorter comparator for REAL leading keys (~10% on speedtest1 fp)
vdbesort.c selects specialized comparators when a sorter's first key field
is proven all-INTEGER (vdbeSorterCompareInt) or all-BINARY-TEXT
(vdbeSorterCompareText); REAL leading keys always take the generic
sqlite3VdbeRecordUnpack() / sqlite3VdbeRecordCompare() path. I would
like to suggest a third comparator for REAL keys using the same typeMask
pattern. Measured on speedtest1 --testset fp --size 100:

Linux x86-64 (perf counters): 9.6% fewer CPU cycles (1.107x
baseline/candidate ratio), 16.2% fewer instructions. cte improves ~5%;
main is unchanged (instruction counts identical to 5 digits).
macOS ARM64 (elapsed time, independent revalidation): median 1.078x
over eight paired runs (~7.2% less time).

The patch is attached at the end of this post. I
understand the team may prefer to reimplement the idea.

What the patch does
The patch (full diff at the end of this post) makes three changes to
vdbesort.c:

1. A new comparator, vdbeSorterCompareReal(). When both records
store their first key field as serial type 7 (an 8-byte big-endian
IEEE-754 value), it loads the two doubles directly from the key buffers,
compares them, and calls the existing vdbeSorterCompareTail() when they
are equal and further key fields exist. This skips
sqlite3VdbeRecordUnpack() / sqlite3VdbeRecordCompare() for the common
case, which is where the measured savings come from.

2. A new SORTER_TYPE_REAL bit in VdbeSorter.typeMask. Like the
existing INTEGER and TEXT bits, it is maintained in
sqlite3VdbeSorterWrite() from the serial type of each added record's
first field, and consulted once per sort in vdbeSorterGetCompare().
Eligibility is therefore decided per sorter, not per comparison: a sorter
that cannot use the fast path selects exactly the same comparator as
today and runs an unchanged instruction stream.

3. A slightly weaker mask invariant than one might expect — this is the
subtle part. The natural rule would mirror SORTER_TYPE_INTEGER:
"every record's first field is serial type 7." That rule is nearly useless
in practice, because the record format stores REAL values with no
fractional part using integer serial types. In the fp testset,
12,996 of the 500,000 REAL values (2.6%) are integral, so an all-type-7
mask essentially never survives; I implemented and measured that variant,
and its fp:100 cycle ratio was 0.997x — the comparator existed but was
never selected. The patch instead tracks "every first field is numeric":

integer serial types keep both the INTEGER and REAL bits alive;
serial type 7 keeps only the REAL bit;
text serial types keep only the TEXT bit (as today);
anything else (NULL, blob) clears the mask (as today).

vdbeSorterGetCompare() tests INTEGER first, so a proven all-integer
sorter still selects vdbeSorterCompareInt() unchanged. A surviving REAL
bit therefore means: every first field is numeric, and at least one is
stored as serial type 7.

Because of that weaker invariant, vdbeSorterCompareReal() must still
expect integer-typed first fields: it checks the two serial-type bytes,
and if either is not 7 — or the payload is a NaN bit pattern, which
SQLite never writes but hostile inputs could contain — it falls back to
the untouched generic comparator, preserving SQLite's exact INTEGER/REAL
comparison semantics. In the fp workload roughly 95% of comparisons take
the direct path. DESC handling, ties, and the arming preconditions
(nAllField<13, default/BINARY collation on the first key, no
KEYINFO_ORDER_BIGNULL) follow the existing INTEGER/TEXT comparators.

