Correctness
speedtest1 --verify intentionally omits floating-point values from its
hash, so these checks lean on byte-identical files and differential output:

On both platforms: --verify hashes match and the baseline/candidate
database files are byte-identical (fp and main); a 300k-row
external-sort differential test (temp_store=FILE, 2 MB cache, 8 sorter
threads; integral/fractional REALs, signed zero, subnormals, ±infinity,
INTEGER/REAL boundary values, NULL, ASC/DESC, NULLS LAST, NOCASE
secondary key, CREATE INDEX, DISTINCT, GROUP BY) produced byte-identical
outputs and database files, with integrity_check ok.
A diagnostic build re-ran the generic comparator after every fast-path
comparison and asserted sign agreement: no disagreements.
Assert-enabled make devtest (Linux): 0 errors out of 977,068 tests.
