uncompressFunc decodes up to five bytes of its BLOB argument as an uncompressed length nOut and passes nOut+1 to sqlite3_malloc, which takes a 32-bit int. The five-byte prefix X'1000000080' decodes to nOut = 2^32. The addition nOut+1 therefore becomes 2^32+1, which converts to 1 at the int parameter, so a one-byte heap block is allocated. zlib uncompress is then called with that tiny buffer and destLen still set to 2^32. The remainder of the argument is a valid deflate stream of one million zero bytes (the body of compress(zeroblob(1000000)) after its original size prefix is stripped), so zlib writes far past the allocated block.

The compress extension registers SQL uncompress from sqlite3_compress_init via sqlite3_create_function. Evaluating the statement below dispatches into uncompressFunc. After the wrapped allocation and the zlib write, the process receives SIGSEGV.

Reproduction
Requires a non-default compress extension.
