Header decode for this input: bytes 10 00 00 00 80 yield nOut = (((((((((0<<7)|0x10)<<7)|0)<<7)|0)<<7)|0)<<7)|0 = 2^32. The high bit on the fifth byte ends the varint, so i == 5 and the zlib payload starts at &pIn[5].

Data flow
sqlite3_compress_init → sqlite3_create_function("uncompress", uncompressFunc) → SQL uncompress(...) → uncompressFunc → sqlite3_malloc → zlib uncompress.

sqlite3_compress_init (ext/misc/compress.c) exposes SQL compress / uncompress after the extension is loaded.
compressFunc (ext/misc/compress.c) builds a 1-to-5-byte size prefix plus a zlib body; the PoC keeps only that body via substr(..., 4) and prepends X'1000000080'.
uncompressFunc (ext/misc/compress.c) reads argv[0] with sqlite3_value_blob / sqlite3_value_bytes, folds up to five bytes into nOut, and does not cap nOut against the allocated type or SQLITE_MAX_ALLOCATION_SIZE.
sqlite3_malloc (src/malloc.c) receives nOut+1 as int. For this header that argument is 1, so a small block is returned and execution continues.
zlib uncompress is told the destination length is still 2^32 and writes the decompressed million-byte zero run past that block. The process then dies with SIGSEGV.

