zipfileUpdate applies UPDATE OR REPLACE that renames one in-memory archive node (pOld) onto another existing name (pOld2). Before those nodes are unlinked it walks pTab->pCsrList and, when pCsr->pCurrent equals pOld or pOld2, assigns pCurrent = pCurrent->pNext once. If the two removed nodes are adjacent, a cursor that was on pOld is left on pOld2. The function then calls zipfileRemoveEntryFromList for both pointers, and that helper frees the ZipfileEntry. bNoop only skips the next zipfileNext advance; it does not clear the now-dangling pCurrent.

UPDATE OR REPLACE z SET name='b.txt' FROM (VALUES(1),(2)) opens a zipfile cursor (zipfileOpen, zipfileFilter) and joins each archive row with two VALUES rows. The first joined row is a.txt; xUpdate (zipfileUpdate) renames it onto b.txt (OR REPLACE). The same statement then continues the scan, so zipfileNext / zipfileColumn run against the cursor that the fixup left on the now-freed pOld2.

Reproduction
Requires zipfile (loaded by default). Run from a writable working directory (it creates poc.zip).
BEGIN;
CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE z USING zipfile('poc.zip');
INSERT INTO z(name, data) VALUES('a.txt', 'AAAA');
INSERT INTO z(name, data) VALUES('b.txt', 'BBBB');
INSERT INTO z(name, data) VALUES('c.txt', 'CCCC');
UPDATE OR REPLACE z SET name='b.txt' FROM (VALUES(1),(2));
COMMIT;

The three inserts build the in-memory list a.txt -> b.txt -> c.txt. The UPDATE FROM keeps that scan cursor on pCsrList while zipfileUpdate frees a.txt and b.txt.

