I found a printf-style argument mismatch in the maintained
tool/sqlite3_rsync.c utility. I reproduced and validated it against SQLite
trunk check-in 11a73d18be23f52c.

The replica-side debug path currently contains:
if( p->zDebugFile ) debugMessage(p, "-> REPLICA_READY\n", iHash);

debugMessage() forwards zFormat and its variadic arguments directly to
vfprintf():
static void debugMessage(SQLiteRsync *p, const char *zFormat, ...){
if( p->zDebugFile ){
if( p->pDebug==0 ){
p->pDebug = fopen(p->zDebugFile, "wb");
}
if( p->pDebug ){
va_list ap;
va_start(ap, zFormat);
vfprintf(p->pDebug, zFormat, ap);
va_end(ap);
fflush(p->pDebug);
}
}
}

The REPLICA_READY format string has no conversions, so the iHash argument
is unused. Ordinary builds do not diagnose the call because debugMessage()
is a project-defined variadic wrapper without a printf-format annotation.

To expose the existing relation to GCC, I temporarily added the following
declaration before the definition:
static void debugMessage(SQLiteRsync *p, const char *zFormat, ...)
__attribute__((format(printf,2,3)));

Then I configured and built the maintained target with strict format checking:
mkdir build && cd build
../configure CFLAGS="-O2 -Wall -Wextra -Wformat=2 -Werror=format-extra-args"
make -j4 sqlite3_rsync

GCC reports:
tool/sqlite3_rsync.c:1676:39: error: too many arguments for format
[-Werror=format-extra-args]

A minimal repair is to remove the unused argument:
- if( p->zDebugFile ) debugMessage(p, "-> REPLICA_READY\n", iHash);
+ if( p->zDebugFile ) debugMessage(p, "-> REPLICA_READY\n");

I validated three build variants at the same revision:
unmodified source:                    build succeeds
temporary format annotation:         build fails at this call
annotation plus one-line repair:      build succeeds

Tested on x86_64 Ubuntu 22.04 with GCC 11.4.0.

I also exercised the real rsync protocol over non-interactive localhost SSH
with separate unmodified and repaired binaries. Both runs returned success and
synchronized the same three-row database into fresh replicas. The two replica
database files were byte-identical. The local debug logs were byte-identical,
and the remote debug logs were byte-identical; both remote logs include:
-> REPLICA_READY

The call was introduced by check-in
c70330668690e7c3, whose
comment says it added debugging features to better visualize the protocol.

This appears to be a minor extra-argument error in a debug-only
printf-style call. It does not affect the database or protocol output
in the tested path. Would removing the unused iHash argument be the
preferred correction?

